# The C sources are edited and compiled on OS/2, where CRLF is the native
# convention and the 1990s editors assume it. A file with bare LF line endings
# looks to them like one enormous line -- json.c would be a single 26 KB line --
# and their fixed line buffers overflow and crash on open. Open Watcom's IDE
# dies on buf.c at 1.6 KB.
#
# -text rather than "text eol=crlf" on purpose: eol=crlf stores LF in the
# repository and only converts on checkout, so a ZIP download or a copy out of
# the web UI would still arrive LF-only. -text means the CRLF bytes are what is
# actually committed, so every route to the target machine delivers them.
*.c -text
*.h -text

# Host-side only, and both actively dislike CRLF: GNU make treats a trailing CR
# as part of the recipe, and the test helpers never leave this machine. Pinned
# so a clone with core.autocrlf=true does not break the build.
Makefile  text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
